# Minicate — Cursor Rules

## Project Context

Minicate is a modern real-time chat application built for a frontend take-home assignment.

The assignment requires:

- Login using phone number and name.
- Automatic registration for a new phone number.
- Search users by phone number or name.
- One-to-one conversations.
- Group conversations.
- Full conversation history.
- Clear sender/receiver message distinction.
- Message timestamps.
- Sending messages.
- Real-time incoming messages without refresh.
- Loading, empty, and error states.
- Smart auto-scroll: scroll to new messages only when the user is already near the bottom.
- A creative responsive landing page.
- Clean, maintainable, production-oriented code.
- Live deployment.

The backend provides REST APIs and explicitly delivers new messages over WebSocket using the `message:new` event.

Do not invent API behavior. Always base API integration on the provided Swagger documentation and observed API responses.

---

## Current Stack

The installed project currently uses:

- Next.js `16.3.1`
- React `19.2.8`
- TypeScript `5`
- Tailwind CSS `4`
- shadcn
- `@shadcn/react`
- `@base-ui/react`
- `lucide-react`
- `clsx`
- `tailwind-merge`
- `class-variance-authority`
- `tw-animate-css`
- `date-fns`

Package manager:

- pnpm `11.6.0`

Before introducing a new dependency, check `package.json`.

If a required library is not installed, prefer an existing project capability first. Only add a dependency when it provides clear value and is appropriate for the assignment.

Do not silently introduce TanStack Query, Zustand, Axios, React Hook Form, Zod, Socket.IO, or other libraries unless they are actually installed or the implementation explicitly requires adding them.

---

# 1. Architecture Principles

Use Next.js App Router.

Keep route files thin.

Prefer this structure:

```text
src/
├── app/
│   ├── layout.tsx
│   ├── page.tsx
│   ├── login/
│   │   └── page.tsx
│   ├── chat/
│   │   └── page.tsx
│   └── ...
├── components/
│   ├── ui/
│   ├── common/
│   └── features/
│       ├── auth/
│       ├── chat/
│       └── landing/
├── hooks/
├── lib/
│   ├── api/
│   ├── websocket/
│   └── utils/
├── services/
├── stores/
├── types/
└── utils/
```

Do not create unnecessary abstraction layers.

Prefer simple, explicit code over premature architecture.

---

# 2. API Rules

The backend base URL must come from an environment variable.

Example:

```env
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=https://frontend-task-chatapp.onrender.com/api
```

Never hardcode API URLs inside components.

Create a single API client/helper for REST requests.

All API calls should live in service modules.

Example:

```text
src/services/auth.service.ts
src/services/users.service.ts
src/services/conversations.service.ts
src/services/messages.service.ts
```

Components must not directly call `fetch()` or REST endpoints.

Do not duplicate endpoint strings across the application.

Use typed request and response models.

Do not use `any`.

If the backend response shape is unclear, inspect the Swagger documentation or actual response before implementing.

---

# 3. Authentication

The login flow is:

1. User enters phone number.
2. User enters/sets their name.
3. API authenticates/registers the user.
4. Store the returned authentication information.
5. Redirect to the chat experience.

A new/unique phone number is automatically registered according to the assignment.

Authentication state should be handled centrally.

Never put authentication tokens directly into random component state.

Never log authentication tokens.

Never commit secrets or tokens.

Use environment variables for configuration.

---

# 4. WebSocket / Real-Time Rules

Real-time messaging is a core feature.

The backend documentation states that messages are delivered through WebSocket using:

```text
message:new
```

Use WebSocket for incoming real-time messages.

Do not replace the WebSocket implementation with polling when the WebSocket API is available.

Separate WebSocket connection logic from UI components.

Prefer:

```text
src/lib/websocket/
```

or:

```text
src/hooks/useChatSocket.ts
```

depending on complexity.

The WebSocket layer should handle:

- Connection.
- Authentication if required by the API.
- Event subscription.
- `message:new`.
- Cleanup/disconnection.
- Reconnection where appropriate.
- Error handling.

Do not create a new WebSocket connection every time a message component renders.

The connection lifecycle must be tied to the authenticated user/session.

Clean up listeners when the component or feature unmounts.

Avoid duplicate event listeners.

Incoming messages should update the current conversation immediately.

If a message belongs to another conversation:

- Update unread state if the UX supports it.
- Do not inject it into the currently open conversation.

Do not blindly append duplicate messages.

Use a stable message identifier when available.

---

# 5. Message Sending

Sending a message should use the backend REST API unless the API explicitly requires another mechanism.

The documented endpoint is:

```text
POST /api/messages
```

Expected request shape includes:

```json
{
  "conversationId": "string",
  "text": "Hello!"
}
```

Do not send empty or whitespace-only messages.

Trim message text before validation/submission.

Disable or prevent submission while an invalid message is being sent.

Handle API errors visibly and gracefully.

Do not assume a successful POST means the UI should blindly append a duplicate if the backend also emits `message:new`.

Design the flow to avoid duplicate messages when both REST response and WebSocket event contain the same message.

---

# 6. Message List

The message list is the highest-priority part of the assignment.

It must clearly distinguish:

- Messages sent by the current user.
- Messages received from other users.

Each message should show a timestamp.

Use `date-fns` for date/time formatting where useful.

Do not put complex message-list business logic directly inside JSX.

Extract logic into hooks/utilities.

---

# 7. Smart Auto-Scroll

The chat must:

- Scroll to the newest message by default.
- Continue following new messages when the user is near the bottom.
- NOT force the user to the bottom when they intentionally scroll upward.

Recommended behavior:

```text
User near bottom
    ↓
New message
    ↓
Auto-scroll

User reading older messages
    ↓
New message
    ↓
Stay in current position
    ↓
Show "new messages" affordance
```

Do not use unconditional:

```ts
scrollIntoView()
```

on every message update.

Detect whether the user is close to the bottom before deciding to scroll.

---

# 8. Chat State

Separate server data from UI state.

Server/API data:

- Users.
- Conversations.
- Messages.

UI state:

- Active conversation.
- Search text.
- Composer text.
- Modal state.
- Sidebar state.
- Mobile navigation state.
- New-message indicator.

Avoid duplicating the same message array in multiple unrelated stores.

Keep a single source of truth where practical.

---

# 9. Search

User search should support the API's documented search capabilities.

Search by:

- Name.
- Phone number.

Do not invent client-side search behavior if the backend provides a search endpoint.

Avoid firing unnecessary requests on every keystroke.

Use debouncing if the API/search UX requires it.

Handle:

- Loading.
- No results.
- Errors.
- Empty query.

---

# 10. Group Conversations

The UI must support creating group conversations with multiple participants.

The group creation flow should:

1. Select multiple users.
2. Optionally provide a group name if supported by the API.
3. Submit the documented request.
4. Open the newly created conversation.

Do not assume fields that are not documented.

Keep participant selection state separate from message state.

---

# 11. Loading, Empty, and Error States

Every async feature must have deliberate states.

Examples:

```text
Loading:
- Skeleton
- Spinner
- Loading placeholder

Empty:
- No conversations
- No search results
- No messages
- No selected conversation

Error:
- API failure
- WebSocket failure
- Message send failure
```

Never leave the user staring at a blank screen with no explanation.

Errors should be understandable to users.

Technical details can be logged for development, but should not be the primary user-facing message.

---

# 12. Component Rules

Prefer small components with one responsibility.

Examples:

```text
ChatLayout
ConversationSidebar
ConversationSearch
ConversationList
ConversationListItem
ChatHeader
MessageList
MessageBubble
MessageComposer
NewMessageIndicator
GroupCreateDialog
```

Avoid a giant `ChatPage.tsx` containing the entire application.

UI components should receive props rather than reaching into unrelated application state.

Keep business logic in hooks/services.

---

# 13. Next.js Rules

Use Server Components by default.

Add:

```tsx
"use client"
```

only when the component actually requires:

- React state.
- Effects.
- Browser APIs.
- Event handlers.
- WebSocket.
- Client-side stores.

The chat application will naturally contain client components.

Do not make the entire application a client component unnecessarily.

---

# 14. TypeScript Rules

Use strict TypeScript.

Never use:

```ts
any
```

Prefer:

```ts
unknown
```

when the shape genuinely is unknown, then narrow it safely.

Define API types explicitly.

Example:

```ts
export interface Message {
  id: string
  conversationId: string
  senderId: string
  text: string
  createdAt: string
}
```

Do not duplicate the same interface in multiple files.

Use domain-based type files:

```text
src/types/auth.ts
src/types/user.ts
src/types/conversation.ts
src/types/message.ts
```

---

# 15. Styling

Minicate uses a modern cyan → blue → violet visual identity.

Primary brand direction:

```text
Cyan → Blue → Violet
```

Use the existing CSS variables/theme system.

Prefer semantic design tokens:

```text
bg-background
text-foreground
bg-primary
text-primary-foreground
border-border
bg-muted
text-muted-foreground
```

Do not scatter arbitrary hex colors throughout components.

Avoid excessive gradients.

Use the brand gradient primarily for:

- Logo.
- Primary CTA.
- Important interactive states.
- Landing-page hero elements.
- Selected/highlighted brand elements.

Keep the chat interface calm and readable.

The message area should prioritize readability over decoration.

---

# 16. Accessibility

Interactive controls must have accessible names.

Buttons containing only icons must use `aria-label`.

Inputs must have labels or appropriate accessible labeling.

Keyboard navigation should work for:

- Search.
- Conversation selection.
- Message composer.
- Send action.
- Dialogs.

Do not use color alone to communicate message ownership or status.

Maintain sufficient contrast.

---

# 17. Responsive Design

The application must work on:

- Desktop.
- Tablet.
- Mobile.

The chat UI should adapt rather than simply shrink.

Recommended mobile behavior:

```text
Mobile
├── Conversation list
└── Chat view
    └── Navigate between them
```

Do not allow the message composer to become inaccessible when the mobile keyboard opens.

Avoid fixed heights that break on small screens.

---

# 18. Performance

Avoid unnecessary renders.

Do not recreate WebSocket connections unnecessarily.

Do not attach duplicate WebSocket listeners.

Avoid expensive operations inside render.

For long conversations, consider message virtualization only if actually needed.

Do not prematurely optimize.

Prioritize correctness and UX first.

---

# 19. Error Handling

API errors must be handled at the service/hook boundary.

Never silently swallow errors.

Use consistent error handling.

Example:

```ts
try {
  // request
} catch (error) {
  // convert to a user-safe error
  // preserve useful debugging information in development
}
```

Never show raw stack traces to users.

If WebSocket disconnects unexpectedly, communicate the connection state appropriately.

---

# 20. Security

Never commit:

- API tokens.
- JWTs.
- Passwords.
- Private keys.
- `.env` secrets.

Do not hardcode the token shown in Swagger examples.

Treat all API data as untrusted input.

Do not use `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` for user-generated messages.

Render message text as plain text.

---

# 21. Git and Code Quality

Keep commits focused.

Use meaningful names.

Remove unused imports and dead code.

Do not leave debug `console.log()` statements in production code.

Before considering a feature complete, run:

```bash
pnpm lint
pnpm build
```

Fix TypeScript and ESLint errors before moving forward.

---

# 22. Assignment-Specific Priorities

Prioritize work in this order:

1. API understanding/documentation.
2. Authentication.
3. Conversation/user search.
4. Conversation creation.
5. Message history.
6. Message sending.
7. WebSocket real-time updates.
8. Smart auto-scroll.
9. Loading/empty/error states.
10. Responsive polish.
11. Creative landing page.
12. README / thought-process write-up.

Do not spend excessive time on the landing page before the core chat works.

The assignment explicitly places the most emphasis on the chat panel, message list, sending, and real-time behavior.

---

# 23. Do Not Invent API Behavior

This is one of the most important project rules.

If the Swagger documentation says something, follow it.

If the API response differs from Swagger:

1. Record the observed response.
2. Determine the safest implementation.
3. Keep the API adapter isolated.
4. Document the inconsistency in the README.

Do not silently build assumptions into UI components.

The service layer should absorb backend-specific quirks where possible.

---

# 24. Development Workflow

For every feature:

```text
1. Inspect API contract
2. Define TypeScript types
3. Implement service/API function
4. Implement hook/state logic
5. Implement UI
6. Add loading/empty/error states
7. Test real API behavior
8. Test responsive behavior
9. Run lint
10. Run build
```

Do not jump directly from API documentation to a large UI component.

---

# 25. Cursor Behavior

When modifying the project:

- Inspect existing files before creating replacements.
- Reuse existing shadcn/UI components where appropriate.
- Do not overwrite working code unnecessarily.
- Do not introduce a new library when the current stack already solves the problem.
- Keep changes scoped to the requested feature.
- Preserve existing naming conventions.
- Prefer incremental changes.
- Explain important architectural changes briefly before making them.
- If API behavior is unclear, ask for the API response/documentation rather than inventing it.
- When implementing WebSocket behavior, verify the actual connection URL, authentication mechanism, event payload, and lifecycle before coding against assumptions.

---

# 26. Definition of Done

A feature is not complete until:

- [ ] TypeScript has no errors.
- [ ] ESLint passes.
- [ ] API calls use the service layer.
- [ ] No `any` was introduced.
- [ ] Loading state exists where needed.
- [ ] Empty state exists where needed.
- [ ] Error state exists where needed.
- [ ] Mobile layout works.
- [ ] Keyboard interaction works where applicable.
- [ ] Real-time behavior does not create duplicate messages.
- [ ] WebSocket listeners are cleaned up.
- [ ] No secrets are committed.
- [ ] `pnpm build` succeeds.
